2: \nL153: arXivLabs is a framework that allows collaborators to develop and share new arXiv features directly on our website.\nL154: \nL155: Both individuals and organizations that work with arXivLabs have embraced and accepted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nL156: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? 【45†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org】.\nL157: \nL158: 【46†Which authors of this paper are endorsers?】 | Disable MathJax (【47†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org】)\nL159: \nL160: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, 【48†member institutions†info.arxiv.org】, , and all contributors.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2610.14849] StateSync-GKR: Design and Evaluation of a Reusable Sparse GKR Engine (https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14849)\nciteturn1view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14849\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 166\nL0: 【0†Search】 【1†Submit】 【2†Donate†info.arxiv.org】 【3†Log in】\nL1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · 【4†Advanced search】\nL5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Cryptography and Security\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 10 Oct 2026 (【5†v1】), last revised 26 Oct 2026 (this version, v2)]\nL9: # Title:StateSync-GKR: Design and Evaluation of a Reusable Sparse GKR Engine\nL10: \nL11: Authors:【6†Jinwook Kim】\nL12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled StateSync-GKR: Design and Evaluation of a Reusable Sparse GKR Engine, by Jinwook Kim\nL14: \nL15: 【7†View PDF】 【8†HTML (experimental)】\nL16: > Abstract:Authenticated state transitions need proof engines that conserve memory, avoid repeated setup, keep every result bound to its request under parallel execution and return complete proofs at service rates. We present StateSync-GKR, a reusable Rust sumcheck/GKR core with a sparse-Merkle frontend, and evaluate release 1.2.0 against release 1.1 measured on the same day on the same instance types, request corpus and latency budgets.\nL17: Sparse layer reduction keeps additional requested allocation 3,242-fold and peak process memory about 89-fold below the engine's dense reference at width 4,096.\nL18: Release 1.2 changes two parts of the software while proof bytes and verifier acceptance stay fixed: reorganized round arithmetic raises prepared proving throughput 2.4 to 2.7-fold on a 192-core bare-metal host, and a new serving path starts each job as soon as a worker is free, bounds queued work and buffered replies, and returns proofs in completion order bound to request identifiers. On a 192-core VM at 1,000 requests/s, 99th-percentile latency to verified acceptance falls from 440 to 34 ms.\nL19: One connection carries 3,300 requests/s with every request inside one second, confirmed in three 60-second runs and a 30-minute run of 5.94 million requests without a latency or queue trend; at 3,400 requests/s reply traffic reaches the provider's documented 5-Gbit/s single-flow limit, and two connections passed 5,000 requests/s in 15-second screens. In single 15-second screens on 48 cores, the highest passing rate rose from 500 to 2,000/s.\nL20: Offered 1.5 times its highest screened one-connection rate under a memory limit, with excess offers held back by the client, the worker held its resident memory near a fifth of that limit and left no submitted request unresolved. For an integrating service, fewer servers deliver the same proof rate with bounded tail latency, and existing verifiers accept the GKR proofs unchanged.\nL21: Comments:  | 167 pages; 12 main figures and a detailed experimental atlas appendix. v2 evaluates release 1.2.0 (new serving path; reorganized prover arithmetic, byte-identical proofs) against same-day release 1.1 runs on the sa